data.content:
**No Thread.Abort migration needed.** Your code uses only `Thread.Start`, `Thread.Join`, and `Thread.Sleep` — none of which are affected by the .NET 6+ `Thread.Abort` breaking change. This code will compile and run correctly as-is on .NET 8.

**Optional modernization** (unrelated to Thread.Abort migration): You could simplify this with `Parallel.ForEach` or `Task.Run` + `Task.WhenAll`, which would also give you easy `CancellationToken` support if you need it later:

```csharp
public async Task RunBatchAsync(List<WorkItem> items,
    CancellationToken token = default)
{
    var tasks = items.Select(item =>
        Task.Run(() => Process(item), token));
    await Task.WhenAll(tasks);
}
```

But again, your existing code works fine on .NET 8 without any changes.
